WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.270
TOWNSEND: Welcome, ladies and gentlemen, to
the Council on Foreign Relations meeting with

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.780
director of national intelligence, my good
friend Jim Clapper. This meeting is part of

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:14.679
the Kenneth A. Moskow Memorial Lecture on
homeland security and counterterrorism, which

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:20.830
honors the memory of Kenneth Moskow, a long-time
CFR member, with a distinguished career in

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:25.620
the intelligence community. Further details
on his life and professional accomplishments

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:31.039
can be found in the booklet of today's meeting.
I'd like to extend a special welcome to Keith

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:38.840
Moskow and to the family and appreciate your
support and your being here today.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.740
So, usual format. I'm going to begin by asking
the questions and then we will make sure I

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:52.830
have left time for members to ask their questions.
You spent a lot of time testifying on Capitol

00:00:52.830 --> 00:01:01.130
Hill in the last couple of days. Last week
you said that when a final accounting is done,

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:07.780
2014 will be the most lethal year for global
terrorism in the&nbsp;forty-five years that the

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:16.090
data has been kept: 13,000 attacks, 31,000
killed. There seemed to be a little, in Washington

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:22.040
terms, kerfuffle with the secretary of state's
statement on the same issue. Why don't you

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.570
speak to that.
CLAPPER: Well, I think the secretary talked

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:31.010
about—I had occasion to speak with him afterwards
and I think he was thinking of a different

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:38.420
context, which of course, context doesn't
lend itself to soundbites in Washington, D.C.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:43.890
And I think what he was thinking about was
the more cataclysmic case in point of the

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:52.570
case with the Cold War. And he's right. In
that context we are a safer country.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:59.750
But I was looking at more the here and now,
what happened in 2014 and kind of what we

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:06.750
project out in the next year, which is the
framework of normally our threat assessment.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:13.189
So just two different contexts, I think.
TOWNSEND: So about&nbsp;ten days ago the president

00:02:13.189 --> 00:02:18.749
hosted the Countering Violent Extremism summit
at the White House. There were state and local

00:02:18.749 --> 00:02:24.079
partners, nonprofit partners, international
partners, in the course of two and a half

00:02:24.079 --> 00:02:27.629
days.
The president sort of steadfastly refused

00:02:27.629 --> 00:02:34.580
to call it Islamic extremism, but the current
threat is not Buddhists or Christians. How

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.011
do you define a threat?
CLAPPER: Well, the way I look at this and

00:02:38.011 --> 00:02:44.079
the way I think about it is, the president
has a much larger agenda. He is speaking to

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:53.040
1.6 billion Muslims around the world. We in
the intelligence community are focused on

00:02:53.040 --> 00:03:00.599
the very, very small, way less than 1 percent
of that 1.6 billion Muslims. So our proclivity

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:08.049
would be to call these people what they call
themselves, you know;&nbsp;IS is the Islamic State.

00:03:08.049 --> 00:03:14.709
So I think our purposes, our missions there,
if you will, are a little bit different, but

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:20.279
in intelligence we need to be as accurate
as we possibly can and call things in many

00:03:20.279 --> 00:03:27.060
cases what they call themselves. And so that's
our focus, as opposed to his, which is much

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.389
broader.
TOWNSEND: Let's talk for a minute. One of

00:03:30.389 --> 00:03:36.059
the great threats and concerns here in the
United States has been about foreign fighters.

00:03:36.059 --> 00:03:43.909
In testimony, you referred to more than 20,000
foreign fighters, 3,400 of whom are Westerners,

00:03:43.909 --> 00:03:50.719
150 of whom are Americans. We've seen cases
of Americans trying to go over, women. There

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:57.510
is a case right now of the British schoolgirls
who folks are trying to find.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:02.459
CLAPPER: The case here in Brooklyn.
TOWNSEND: The case here in Brooklyn. Despite

00:04:02.459 --> 00:04:06.379
airstrikes, why are the numbers of foreign
fighters not just continuing but, according

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:10.189
to the head of the National Counterterrorism
Center, they're increasing?

00:04:10.189 --> 00:04:15.689
CLAPPER: They are, and it' a great concern.
And I was just in Europe a month or so ago,

00:04:15.689 --> 00:04:23.849
in fact in Paris and Brussels, and it is very
much on the minds of European countries. The

00:04:23.849 --> 00:04:29.650
actual number I quoted in open testimony with
the Senate Armed Services Committee last Thursday

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:34.900
was about 180 Americans.
Now when I say that, that is those who have

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:38.460
attempted to go and never got there, those
that went and got killed, those that went

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:43.250
and fought and went someplace else, and those
who have come back, which is a much lesser

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:50.150
number. So when you think of our population,
315 million, whatever it is, and a relatively

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:56.240
small number of people in contrast to the
European countries, where it is overwhelming

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.310
in terms of their ability to monitor and surveil
these people.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:07.720
So it is a tremendous problem, you know. The
question is why, you know, what is the attraction.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:13.069
I don't think there's any single explanation
for it. There's a combination of factors,

00:05:13.069 --> 00:05:20.400
emotional, ideological, whatever. But this
is a very bothersome concern and of course

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:31.830
it gives rise somewhat to probably the most
frequent threat that we here worry about,

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:41.150
which is homegrown violent extremists.
ISIL particularly is very slick, very sophisticated,

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:49.040
proselytizing and recruiting, and they are
very astute users of social media. So this

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.350
is a challenge for us as it is in Europe.
TOWNSEND: Director, you mentioned the small

00:05:54.350 --> 00:06:00.569
number that come back. What do you estimate,
of 150 Americans, foreign fighters, what number

00:06:00.569 --> 00:06:03.370
of them do you believe have gone and actually
come back?

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:10.139
CLAPPER: On the order of&nbsp;forty or so, and
many of those are not—don't have nefarious

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:16.189
purposes. We have since found they went for
humanitarian purposes or some other reason

00:06:16.189 --> 00:06:21.680
that don't relate to plotting. In fact, at
this point we are not aware of any of these

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:27.819
people who returned who are engaged in plotting.
TOWNSEND: But there are plenty of people here,

00:06:27.819 --> 00:06:32.490
and those listening, I suspect, who are saying,
but wait a minute. If you know they went,

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.810
how is it they were able to come back, and
should that be a concern to us.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:42.250
CLAPPER: Well, if they come back and they
are not involved in plotting or don't have

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:48.039
a nefarious purpose, then that's their right
and privilege as American citizens to come

00:06:48.039 --> 00:06:50.969
back.
TOWNSEND: Now we've heard in the past about

00:06:50.969 --> 00:06:57.789
another group, not ISIS but the Khorasan group
in Syria, an al-Qaeda affiliate, being a direct

00:06:57.789 --> 00:07:02.430
threat to the United States. What is the status
of our efforts against the Khorasan group?

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:08.500
CLAPPER: Well, we are watching them as carefully
as we can. This is an expatriate group that

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:15.009
found its way from Afghanistan, Pakistan.
In fact, Khorasan refers to a region there.

00:07:15.009 --> 00:07:22.180
And they are very focused on plots against
the West and then particularly us. So we're

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:27.479
watching them very carefully.
TOWNSEND: Let me ask you while on the subject

00:07:27.479 --> 00:07:36.069
of Syria, is it, in your judgment, possible
to defeat ISIS in Syria without also targeting

00:07:36.069 --> 00:07:40.539
Assad?
CLAPPER: Well, I think the way we are thinking

00:07:40.539 --> 00:07:49.530
about it now is, what is the priority. And
the priority&nbsp;for us right now is ISIL, acknowledging

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:55.740
the fact that at some point Assad has got
to go because, as particularly many in Europe

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:03.680
feel, that the magnet for all this extremism
that has found its way to Syria is because

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:07.521
of him.
So I think there's certainly recognition of

00:08:07.521 --> 00:08:12.729
that but I think the focus from a priority
standpoint right now is on ISIL.

00:08:12.729 --> 00:08:18.800
TOWNSEND: But that's not necessarily the view
of our Arab allies, who, while they are committed

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:24.659
to the air effort, if we want them to contribute
ground forces, want Assad to go and want us

00:08:24.659 --> 00:08:29.840
to target him as well. So how do you keep
the coalition together while the U.S. may

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:33.680
want to do it sequentially when your Arab
allies want you to do it at the same time?

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:40.560
CLAPPER: Well, this is probably, you know,
this fits gets into the policy arena and the

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.470
metaphor I like to use is that the intelligence
community is down in the engine room shoveling

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.810
intelligence coal and the people on the bridge
decide things like that.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:53.190
(LAUGHTER)
TOWNSEND: That was an elegant avoidance of

00:08:53.190 --> 00:09:01.000
the question. Let's talk about FSA for a minute.
Clearly there has been a policy decision to

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:08.330
train FSA. Ray Odierno, the chairman, the
Army Chief of Staff, last week told Wolf Blitzer

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:15.440
that the Army, the U.S. military will begin
to train the FSA in March.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:22.390
CLAPPER: By FSA you mean Free Syrian Army?
TOWNSEND: That's right. The Syrian opposition.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:27.811
Are we confident about vetting in terms from
an intelligence perspective those that we

00:09:27.811 --> 00:09:30.040
are training so that they won't have to be
a threat...

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:35.860
CLAPPER: Well, the challenge here is going
to be the time it's going to take to recruit,

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:42.890
vet and then train people, and then get up
to a sufficient military center of mass, if

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:48.960
you will, to have real impact. So the issue,
at least for me as I watch this, is the time

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:54.450
it's going to take to get sufficient horsepower,
firepower, however you want to characterize

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.990
it, that would have a significant impact on
the battlefield.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:03.290
TOWNSEND: Now let me ask you about, you know,
the consequence of this five year-plus civil

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:09.080
war that's been going on, it's the largest
number of refugees and internally displaced

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:13.120
persons since World War II.
CLAPPER: Right. You read my oral statement

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:18.460
very carefully.
TOWNSEND: From a counterterror perspective,

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:23.190
I worry that's also the largest recruiting
pool you could possibly create for the future

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:29.790
of ISIS and al-Qaeda. How do we position ourselves
to try and thwart that?

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:33.791
CLAPPER: That is&nbsp;a great concern and is something
that doesn't get a lot of attention. There

00:10:33.791 --> 00:10:41.680
are something on the order of 11.4 million
displaced people just from Syria alone, which

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:49.500
has literally flooded the adjacent countries.
This is a huge strain on the likes of Lebanon,

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:56.630
Jordan and Turkey, for example. And so as
best they can to try and cope with this onslaught

00:10:56.630 --> 00:11:01.820
of refugees, and of course you can imagine
some of the conditions, living conditions,

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:08.640
which of course just gives rise to—creates
the environment for recruiting.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:16.640
And so that's a great concern that I have
and it is hard for us to reach directly into

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:22.332
these refugee camps. I will say, though, that
one of the things that I think we have awakened

00:11:22.332 --> 00:11:29.440
to is the need for a very robust effort at
the counter narrative, and this is particularly

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:35.920
so with ISIL, since they are very, as I said
before, very slick, very sophisticated at

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:44.360
appealing to, you know, a fairly large segment
of people, as they will in these refugee camps.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:50.940
TOWNSEND: So to use your analogy, you are
shoveling the intel into the system below

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:56.641
deck. Is this an issue that the policymakers
are grappling with, the refugee camps and

00:11:56.641 --> 00:12:03.320
the potential future for recruitment?
CLAPPER: Well, I think the policy apparatus,

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:07.350
the policy-making community in Washington
is dealing with this whole potpourri, this

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:18.370
myriad of issues in Washington. It always
interests me, you know, when I watch TV and

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:27.090
how simple things are, and somehow in the
situation room they ain't so simple. And this

00:12:27.090 --> 00:12:36.040
is the kind of dilemma we have today, the
diversity of crises and challenges we have

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:39.055
around the world.
I've been, in one capacity or another, in

00:12:39.055 --> 00:12:44.530
the intel business for&nbsp;fifty-two years and
I don't remember a time when we had been beset

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:50.550
by more crises and challenges around the world,
and a diversity of these crises and challenges

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:54.510
than we have today.
TOWNSEND: So we can't sort of talk about Syria

00:12:54.510 --> 00:13:00.920
without also talking about Iran. Senator Reed,
during your testimony, asked you the question

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:08.340
if nuclear negotiations fail, if we were to
intervene against Assad in Syria, what is

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:14.000
your assessment based on the intelligence
about the likelihood Iran might become more

00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:18.540
aggressive against U.S. interests, whether
in Iraq or around the world.

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:23.980
CLAPPER: Well, I honestly don't know, you
know. People want clairvoyance, you've got

00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:29.130
to spend a lot more money on intelligence,
I guess. I don't know, you know, what they'll

00:13:29.130 --> 00:13:33.330
do if there's not a deal. I know they want
one very badly because they want—in the

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:41.400
worst way, want sanctions relief.
We are at a very delicate time right now with

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:47.260
respect to negotiations, and so I don't know
how that will come out. I'm not sure just

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:51.180
what that would do if there is&nbsp;not a deal,
how that would—what impact that would have

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:58.190
on Iranian behavior. I don't know that they
would change their basic policies, particularly

00:13:58.190 --> 00:14:02.390
as they pertain to regional influence. I don't
think it would change it much.

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:06.590
I think they would continue to do as they
have, and clearly they are—they have big

00:14:06.590 --> 00:14:16.790
interest in the outcome of things in Iraq.
They very much consistently wanted a Shia-friendly

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:20.960
government that they can influence in Baghdad.
And so that will continue.

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:28.340
I think they will continue sustaining their
surrogates, Hezbollah. They have an interest

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:37.120
in Syria. So I don't see that changing, regardless
of the outcome of the negotiations. In fact,

00:14:37.120 --> 00:14:41.750
I made the point to Senator Reed, for whatever
reason those are kind of separate and distinct

00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:46.210
right now. They really haven't made the connection.
TOWNSEND: And I found that interesting. The

00:14:46.210 --> 00:14:53.170
word you use is they have compartmentalized,
right? The problem is, when they care—we

00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:58.010
saw during the Iraq war when we had U.S. troops
there, they were not the least bit shy when

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:05.200
they didn't like U.S. policy, about targeting
our military. But you don't think they will

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:10.010
get more aggressive against us?
CLAPPER: The issue for them, for the Iranians,

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:17.870
is sustaining their control, influence, over
the Shia militia. There are somewhere in the

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:23.150
neighborhood of seven or eight major—there
may be more, but the major Shia militia organizations

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:31.800
in Iran, and I think as long as the Iranians
perceive that we are doing what comports with

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:37.950
their objective, which is eliminate ISIL,
as long as we are on sort of a parallel course

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:41.750
there, they will do what they can to control
the militias.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:48.360
And of course they have long harbored resentment
of the U.S. presence. They certainly did when

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:54.830
we were there and they do now and they, you
know, look at us as—their rhetoric is that

00:15:54.830 --> 00:16:03.390
we are occupiers. And so it's a concern of
mine as to whether while we think we've messaged

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:09.680
the Iranians about the militias, that's not
to say that they have perfect command and

00:16:09.680 --> 00:16:13.160
control over all—they, the Iranians, have
perfect command and control over the activities

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:20.420
of each militia or each militia member.
TOWNSEND: So speaking of the groups that the

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:26.760
Iranians influence and control, let's move
south to Yemen. Was it an intelligence failure

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:30.650
or did the intelligence community predict
the Houthi takeover of Yemen?

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:34.280
CLAPPER: Well, another old saw of intelligence.
You know, the first week in intelligence school

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:40.400
you learn there are only two conditions in
life. There is policy success or there is

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:46.510
intel failure. There is no other condition
in life.

00:16:46.510 --> 00:16:49.940
(LAUGHTER)
No, it wasn't a failure, and anyone who is,

00:16:49.940 --> 00:16:56.600
you know, remotely acquainted with the history
of politics in Yemen and its history understands

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:04.130
that the governmental institutions there have
always been very, very fragile. And so as

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:10.010
the Houthis expanded their area of influence
and as they infiltrated, particularly in the

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:17.260
Sanaa, infiltrated into the ministries of
the government, infiltrated the intelligence

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:21.380
and security organizations, it was pretty
clear where things were going and it was just

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:24.800
a question of time.
TOWNSEND: And regarding timing, it has been

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:29.600
said to me it was sort of the dog that caught
the bus unexpectedly, but it fell, that the

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:33.280
Houthis gained power more quickly than...
CLAPPER: Well, yes, that's a great question.

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:38.450
And here's where the Houthis and the ISIL
have something in common. When it comes time

00:17:38.450 --> 00:17:45.380
to govern, they find out that that is not
inconsequential undertaking, that's a different

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:51.670
proposition. And the Houthis are discovering
that in Yemen. All of a sudden now, you know,

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:57.580
like it or not they are responsible for the
economy there, which is in extremis. And similarly,

00:17:57.580 --> 00:18:04.750
ISIL is straining to provide governance and
municipal facilities in a place as large as

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:12.220
Mosul, for example. So these are two common
phenomena that both these groups are experiencing.

00:18:12.220 --> 00:18:17.500
TOWNSEND: So let me ask you, there was a recent—there
was a development this morning. The Houthi

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:23.360
had taken captive a senior Saudi diplomat.
He's been in custody for some time. They just

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:28.190
released the Saudi diplomat this morning.
Are we to read into that...

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:32.550
CLAPPER: Being held by the Houthis or by AQAP?
I thought he was held by the AQAP.

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:38.970
TOWNSEND: My question to you, what are we
to read from the release? If he was released

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:46.820
by AQAP—I think you are right, I'm sorry—are
we to be concerned? Should we be concerned

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:50.590
about Saudi interaction with extremist groups
in Yemen?

00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:56.700
CLAPPER: Well, I don't know. I honestly don't
know what to read into that. We will have

00:18:56.700 --> 00:19:06.890
to sort that out to determine the significance.
Normally, you know, AQAP, AQAM, these groups

00:19:06.890 --> 00:19:16.870
will use kidnapping as a way of raising revenue.
So exactly what they had in mind there I don't

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:20.560
know. We will have to sort that out a bit.
TOWNSEND: Well, we know the Saudis have had

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:25.560
an incredible relationship and intelligence
insight into AQAP. They have helped us thwart

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:30.810
attacks. They have been incredible allies,
and so clearly they have some insight...

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:38.260
CLAPPER: Well, they do, and of course they
are very concerned about the rise of the Houthis,

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:49.091
a Shia group, which has certainly relations
with and gets support from the Iranians. And

00:19:49.091 --> 00:19:56.630
so in the minds of some, this maybe conjures
up another chapter of Hezbollah. I don't think

00:19:56.630 --> 00:20:03.320
that's the case. I think the Houthis are much
more independent than that, but the Saudis

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:08.600
classically have been very concerned about
the Houthis right on their border.

00:20:08.600 --> 00:20:15.310
And so the emergence of the Houthis as a dominant
political force in Yemen is a matter of great

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:21.650
concern. I would emphasize what you said,
Fran, about what tremendous partners the Saudis

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:28.450
have been and are.
TOWNSEND: So let's move now to Russia. There's

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:34.750
a whole host of issues between Russia and
Ukraine, but let me start. Nemtsov, an outspoken

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:41.850
opposition figure against Putin, was assassinated
in broad daylight. It just so happened a snowplow

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:47.380
blocked the camera that would've captured
the whole thing. Is there any chance that

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:51.400
this was not perpetrated by Putin and his
allies inside Russia?

00:20:51.400 --> 00:21:01.800
CLAPPER: Well, that's, you know, an obvious
theory. There are those—some Russian Kremlin

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:09.290
officials have suggested that we did it just
to create hate and discontent in the political

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:17.940
fabric of Russia. So there is a long tradition
of that sort of thing. You know, to paraphrase

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:24.750
Stalin, when you have a problem, get rid of
the man. No man, no problem. So I don't know.

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:33.620
We don't know. We don't have any unique insight
as to who did this, and I would venture the

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:38.860
history has been we never do find out. Although
I am sure that Putin himself personally is

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:41.850
going to investigate.
(LAUGHTER)

00:21:41.850 --> 00:21:49.100
TOWNSEND: So you were asked during your hearing
by Senator McCain, based on the intelligence

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:56.950
assessment, we see the Russians arming their
forces in Eastern Ukraine. Should we, and

00:21:56.950 --> 00:22:01.230
what is the discussion within the administration
on arming the Ukrainians?

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:09.620
CLAPPER: Of course that's a policy thing.
But I was kind of prompted, I guess, you know,

00:22:09.620 --> 00:22:15.940
what's my own view. By the way, the Russians
are doing more than just providing arms. They

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:23.830
have moved units, particularly last August,
and really reversed the, you know, battlefield

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:29.440
situation there when the Ukrainians were really
moving east and the Russians stepped in and

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:34.720
stopped that.
I was recently in Kiev about a month or so

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:44.550
ago and I was, you know, quite struck with
the intensity with which the Ukrainians look

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:52.680
at this. And so I look at it more as, you
know, I have been an advocate for arming them

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:59.710
with legal defensive weapons, more to bolster
their resolve and bolster their morale that,

00:22:59.710 --> 00:23:05.900
you know, we are with them.
Again, this is—I will reiterate now what

00:23:05.900 --> 00:23:12.059
I said last Thursday, that this is not company
policy. This is just me, Jimmy Clapper, speaking,

00:23:12.059 --> 00:23:19.270
and this is something that is in deliberation
right now among the policy community. It won't

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:24.330
be an intel call.
TOWNSEND: So there is no question Russia has

00:23:24.330 --> 00:23:31.600
been persistently aggressive. Crimea, now
Eastern Ukraine. There are those who would

00:23:31.600 --> 00:23:38.210
say that there is also intelligence suggesting
further aggression in the former Eastern European

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:42.270
states—Latvia, Estonia. How concerned are
you about that and how likely do you believe

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:47.640
that is?
CLAPPER: Well, first of all I think, you know,

00:23:47.640 --> 00:23:53.680
what motivates, what drives Putin, what's
his worldview, and I think he is somewhat

00:23:53.680 --> 00:24:03.090
of a throwback to the halcyon days of the
great Russian Empire. That's kind of the way

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:09.370
he looks at things. And so on the 22nd of
February last year, when Yanukovych just kind

00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:15.120
of left, suddenly, Ukraine, I think he saw
an opportunity there which he'd been waiting

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:23.309
for, first to take back the Crimea, which,
you know, has always been in his mind a tremendous

00:24:23.309 --> 00:24:29.110
injustice. And of course he considers the
collapse of the Soviet Union one of the greatest

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:32.610
geopolitical disasters in the history of the
world.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:38.050
And so you kind of see where he's coming from.
And particularly with the special relationship

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:44.500
between Russia and Ukraine, which they refer
to as "little Ukraine". And so this is another

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:53.290
occasion not unlike Georgia. The number one
foreign policy goal of Russia is control of

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:59.809
the former Soviet space. So that's what they
did in Georgia and so it is again here in

00:24:59.809 --> 00:25:03.240
Ukraine.
Now&nbsp;I do think there is a difference, even

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:09.510
in their mind, with the Baltic states because,
unlike Ukraine of course, they are NATO members.

00:25:09.510 --> 00:25:16.620
So you have the article 5 thing, which I think
does serve as a deterrent. That's not to say,

00:25:16.620 --> 00:25:24.380
though, that I think the Russians won't be
doing, you know, soft power kinds of things—espionage,

00:25:24.380 --> 00:25:29.010
information operations, propaganda, this sort
of thing which you can, you know, look at

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:33.450
Moldova or Transnistria, and they will continue
to do that.

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:40.760
TOWNSEND: So last week the president announced
the establishment of the Cyber Threat Intelligence

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:45.480
Center. It is by my count will be the sixth
cyber center in the government.

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:51.110
CLAPPER: Actually there's more than that..
TOWNSEND: OK. Well, however many there are,

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:55.990
why is this one necessary and what function
does it perform that's not redundant?

00:25:55.990 --> 00:26:06.500
CLAPPER: Well, I think for one, cyber pervades
so many aspects of our lives and it pervades

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:14.140
so much of what we do in intelligence these
days. So what this element is intended to

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:20.540
do, and it's very small, you know, it's not
going to be that many people. And what I think

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:27.650
we can do is to bring to bear more integration
within the intelligence community and our

00:26:27.650 --> 00:26:33.330
reporting on cyber threats.
And I liken it to what we now do with the

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:41.331
president's daily brief, where we have responsibility
for putting together the ultimate word, if

00:26:41.331 --> 00:26:47.270
you will, by integrating the inputs and views
of the entire intelligence community. And

00:26:47.270 --> 00:26:53.720
that's our intent with this, our modest intent
with this group is to do a better job of integrating

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:58.740
the intelligence picture with respect to cyber
threats.

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:05.080
So we are in the midst now of—I say we,
my deputy Stephanie O'Sullivan and I—are

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:10.360
in the midst of making a lot of parish calls
among all these groups, all of whom are concerned

00:27:10.360 --> 00:27:14.500
and threatened and all that sort of thing,
just to try to allay concerns because the

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:20.710
objectives here, I think, are pretty modest
but I think we can make some value-added contribution

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:26.630
in terms of better job of an integrated threat
picture with respect to cyber.

00:27:26.630 --> 00:27:32.160
TOWNSEND: So, can't speak about cyber these
days without talking about the recent Sony

00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:39.120
attack. There has been much sort of theorizing
and dispute out there in the media. How confident

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:43.420
are you that it was appropriately attributed
to North Korea?

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:47.610
CLAPPER: Very confident.
TOWNSEND: I want you to share with the group

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:53.801
because you had the distinct honor to go and
get the captives that were released. It's

00:27:53.801 --> 00:27:57.490
sort of a fascinating story if you are willing
to share it.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:28:03.820
CLAPPER: Well, yes. And coincidentally of
course, the attack occurred against Sony about

00:28:03.820 --> 00:28:11.330
a week or two after I was there, so I probably
won't be going back. There had been, you know,

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:21.760
some back and forth with the North Koreans
about, you know, releasing the other two citizens.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:27.500
We had one, Jeffrey Fowle, was released in
October. The difference here was he was not

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:32.010
yet in the North Korean penal system.
And of course typically the North Koreans

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:37.920
like to secure, if they can, some concessions.
And so there was some back and forth about

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:50.420
a presidential envoy coming to Pyongyang to
secure the release of our two citizens. And

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:57.020
the North Koreans wanted someone who was a
member, a current member of government, and

00:28:57.020 --> 00:29:02.800
a member of the National Security Council.
Somehow—I'm not sure where the suggestion

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:08.690
came from, whether it was from them or the
White House, but my name came up, and amazingly,

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:16.160
the North Koreans agreed. I had served in
the Republic of Korea in the early '80s while

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:21.470
I was on active duty as a director of intelligence
for U.S. forces Korea, and kind of followed

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:31.820
developments on the peninsula ever since.
So they, interestingly enough, bought into

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:37.250
my being the envoy. I thought actually the
New York Times had the best explanation of

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:45.090
why the DNI: gruff, blunt, a relic of the
Cold War, ideal for North Korea.

00:29:45.090 --> 00:29:52.410
(LAUGHTER)
So I was on a trip, in fact I was in Canada,

00:29:52.410 --> 00:30:01.450
and was in Ottawa and I was due to come here
to speak here at CFR and as well do a taping

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:05.880
with Charlie Rose, which I did this morning.
So I'm making up for that now.

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:12.960
Anyway, I got a summons from Susan Rice saying,
you need to hustle back, so I went to a meeting,

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:19.010
flew back from Ottawa early, joined the meeting
at the White House in which we were discussing

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:26.700
things like don't bow, don't take flowers,
don't smile, you know. Lots of useful policy

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.490
things like that.
(LAUGHTER)

00:30:28.490 --> 00:30:35.720
Anyway, I ran home and repacked my bag and
back out to Andrews and left about 2 o'clock—this

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:41.580
is November 4th, I think, and we had some
airplane challenges on the way out there.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:49.470
And finally arrived on the night of November
7th in Pyongyang and went immediately into

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:57.200
dialogue with the North Koreans.
I was met by a political four-star, General

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:05.530
Kim who is a minister of state security. And
so we had this long slow roll in a limo from

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:11.809
Sunan airport downtown to Pyongyang to the
state guesthouse. And we immediately began

00:31:11.809 --> 00:31:21.000
deploying each other's talking points in the
car. I immediately got a little hinky about

00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:26.150
I wasn't getting any assurances that we were
going to get our two citizens back.

00:31:26.150 --> 00:31:30.951
So they dropped us at the state guesthouse,
which is a very nice place, and then we were

00:31:30.951 --> 00:31:39.559
met by the official who touted himself as
my counterpart. He was another General Kim,

00:31:39.559 --> 00:31:45.940
a military four-star Kim who is director of
what's called a Reconnaissance General Bureau,

00:31:45.940 --> 00:31:51.330
which is an amalgam between their intelligence
and special forces.

00:31:51.330 --> 00:31:59.130
Then back in the limo back in downtown Pyongyang
to a restaurant, a private restaurant which

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:05.100
interestingly enough was over a bowling alley.
In fact I remember, I was trying to make light

00:32:05.100 --> 00:32:11.640
conversation—which they don't do—"oh,
people in the DPRK bowl?" and he wasn't having

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:12.640
it.
(LAUGHTER)

00:32:12.640 --> 00:32:18.200
Anyway, we went to the restaurant and it was
very nice, and having served in Korea and

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:23.420
developed a liking for Korean food, and this
marvelous twelve-course meal and the best

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:29.049
kimchee I've ever eaten in my life was in
North Korea. Unfortunately, it wasn't a very

00:32:29.049 --> 00:32:33.030
enjoyable because—pretty terse&nbsp;conversation,
pretty tense and a lot of arguing, you know,

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:37.640
pointing at each other's chest and that sort
of thing.

00:32:37.640 --> 00:32:40.140
TOWNSEND: Do we think that's why you got stuck
with&nbsp;the check?

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:45.760
CLAPPER: They don't like us. They like our
money, you know, I guess. Anyway, so at the

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:50.310
end of the evening I did have a letter from
the president, which didn't say a great deal

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:56.450
other than just, you know, officially authenticating
me as his representative and, you know, it

00:32:56.450 --> 00:33:00.560
would be viewed by the U.S. government as
a positive gesture if they released our two

00:33:00.560 --> 00:33:05.549
citizens.
Since they didn't hand me a schedule or agenda

00:33:05.549 --> 00:33:13.799
or anything when I got there, we deployed
the letter with the RGB director, and back

00:33:13.799 --> 00:33:22.380
to the state guesthouse. The next day we just
kind of hung out, waiting around for them

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:28.260
to do something. We had a doctor with us and
I did ask if the doctor could visit the two

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:33.990
citizens, which they did agree to, although
instructed the doctor not to say anything

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:39.150
to either of our citizens about the possibility
of them being released.

00:33:39.150 --> 00:33:45.080
So about 11 o'clock we're just hanging out,
and it's not like you can call a cab or something

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:51.900
and drive around. This representative of the
minister of state security came to the state

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:57.350
guesthouse and informed us that I had been
demoted, that they no longer considered me

00:33:57.350 --> 00:34:05.910
a presidential envoy, since I was there simply
to, you know, gain the release of our two

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:10.399
criminals. Moreover, they could not any longer
guarantee the safety and security of me or

00:34:10.399 --> 00:34:18.119
my small party. Thanks.
So what do you do when you're killing time?

00:34:18.119 --> 00:34:23.970
So we had lunch. Hung around, and hung around.
About 3 o'clock, 3:15 in the afternoon, the

00:34:23.970 --> 00:34:29.329
same gentleman came back and said we had&nbsp;twenty
minutes to pack up, check out and get out

00:34:29.329 --> 00:34:34.759
of here, we're leaving. So we pile in the
vehicles. Took us downtown to the Koryo hotel,

00:34:34.759 --> 00:34:41.419
which is a pretty nice hotel in Pyongyang.
Escorted us upstairs to a room about half

00:34:41.419 --> 00:34:50.069
the size of this room and in the middle of
the room was a racetrack-shaped table.

00:34:50.069 --> 00:34:57.380
On one side were three or four North Korean
officials who were, I found out later, representatives

00:34:57.380 --> 00:35:01.060
of the North Korean prosecutor's office. On
the other side were three or four chairs for

00:35:01.060 --> 00:35:07.390
me and a couple of people with me. So we were
just sitting there. It seemed endless but

00:35:07.390 --> 00:35:11.479
it was probably only&nbsp;twenty minutes, and
no one is saying anything, can hear a pin

00:35:11.479 --> 00:35:14.130
drop, and they're staring at us and we are
staring at them.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:19.910
And that's the first time I actually saw our
two citizens, Bae and Miller, who were in

00:35:19.910 --> 00:35:26.920
their prison garb. So we didn't talk to them
or anything. And then finally the door opens

00:35:26.920 --> 00:35:32.100
and in enters the aforementioned minister
of state security, the gentleman that had

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:36.890
met me the night before. We all stand up like
he's a judge or something and he comes in

00:35:36.890 --> 00:35:43.390
and they read a proclamation, which included
a litany of the heinous crimes that our two

00:35:43.390 --> 00:35:50.779
citizens had rendered and performed, and the
generosity of the DPRK government for allowing—that

00:35:50.779 --> 00:35:55.980
they would grant amnesty at the request of
the president.

00:35:55.980 --> 00:36:01.640
Of course that was translated and at the end
of it Minister Kim turned to me and said,

00:36:01.640 --> 00:36:05.099
"well, I hope we can have more dialogue in
the future but not about your criminals".

00:36:05.099 --> 00:36:10.579
And I said thanks. And they all walked out
with our guys and they changed clothes in

00:36:10.579 --> 00:36:17.240
the hotel and out to the airport and we left.
And I must say—we had to stop at several

00:36:17.240 --> 00:36:25.739
places on the way back but got to McChord
Air Force Base about 8:30&nbsp;Saturday night,

00:36:25.739 --> 00:36:31.769
the 8th, I guess it was. And I went up in
the cockpit and just watched the family reunions,

00:36:31.769 --> 00:36:38.950
which was actually gratifying and very emotional
to watch that. And right after that got a

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:47.369
call from the president thanking me and my
team for a successful mission. So had a gin

00:36:47.369 --> 00:36:50.579
and tonic and went to bed for a while. That
was it.

00:36:50.579 --> 00:36:53.470
(LAUGHTER)
TOWNSEND: So I've got two more quick questions

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:58.970
about cyber and then I'm going to open it
up to the members. One, is it in your mind

00:36:58.970 --> 00:37:03.349
possible that North Korea could have pulled
off the Sony cyber attack without the help

00:37:03.349 --> 00:37:07.269
of either Iran or Russia or China or some
other state power?

00:37:07.269 --> 00:37:13.779
CLAPPER: Oh, yes. They are not at the same
level of sophistication and capability as,

00:37:13.779 --> 00:37:20.569
say, the top tier, which means Russia and
China, but they engage in cyber exploitation,

00:37:20.569 --> 00:37:29.039
et cetera, you know, constantly against&nbsp;the
Republic of&nbsp;Korea.

00:37:29.039 --> 00:37:37.780
So they are astute at this. They have a whole
infrastructure, much of which is under the

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:45.249
auspices of the aforementioned Reconnaissance
General Bureau and my good friend General

00:37:45.249 --> 00:37:51.160
Kim. So they are quite capable.
TOWNSEND: Let me ask you. We've seen numerous

00:37:51.160 --> 00:37:56.260
public reports about DDoS, dedicated denial
of service, attacks against U.S. financial

00:37:56.260 --> 00:38:01.849
institutions. What is the likelihood in your
judgment, based on the intelligence, of a

00:38:01.849 --> 00:38:06.300
Sony or Aramco-type of destructive attack
against U.S. financial interests?

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:13.150
CLAPPER: I spoke to that in my oral statement,
that the situation we are in in cyber is—I

00:38:13.150 --> 00:38:20.680
think we attach less likelihood to the notion
of a, you know, cyber Armageddon. That's not

00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:25.140
to say it's impossible. Sure, it is possible,
but less likely.

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:32.630
Instead what we are faced with is this sort
of insidious growth where cyber actors do

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:40.119
increasingly more nefarious things in kind
of a gradual incremental way. So it's not

00:38:40.119 --> 00:38:46.440
going to be some dramatic thing but rather
a gradual increase.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:52.140
So last year, as I pointed out, was the first
time we had destructive cyber attacks on our

00:38:52.140 --> 00:39:00.080
soil, first with the Las Vegas casino Sands
Corporation, and then of course with the Sony

00:39:00.080 --> 00:39:11.700
attack. If you go back and sort of trace the
trendlines in cyber, phishing, DDoS, and then—so

00:39:11.700 --> 00:39:18.789
what's next? Well, deletion or manipulation,
which of course affects the integrity of the

00:39:18.789 --> 00:39:23.960
data, is probably next.
TOWNSEND: Pretty scary stuff. OK. At this

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:28.210
time I would like to invite members to join
our conversation with questions. A reminder

00:39:28.210 --> 00:39:32.940
this meeting is on the record. Wait for the
microphone, speak directly into it, please

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:36.960
stand and then state your name and affiliation.
Please limit yourself to one question and

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:44.819
keep it concise to allow as many members as
possible to speak. Let's start here.

00:39:44.819 --> 00:39:59.299
QUESTION: Thank you. Director, my name is
Roland Paul, I'm a lawyer, I've been in government

00:39:59.299 --> 00:40:00.299
a couple of times. Absolutely thrilling story
about your trip to North Korea, I might add.&nbsp;My

00:40:00.299 --> 00:40:01.759
question relates to Syria. According to the
press there are 40,000 to 80,000 Syrian rebels

00:40:01.759 --> 00:40:20.650
who are not affiliated with ISIL or al-Nusra.
Could you correct that order of magnitude,

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:25.680
and also tell us what might be the U.S. government
relationship with those groups?

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:33.380
CLAPPER: Well, first of all, these numbers
and estimates get tossed around quite a bit.

00:40:33.380 --> 00:40:38.599
I was asked about that during my hearing with
the Senate Armed Services Committee. So I

00:40:38.599 --> 00:40:46.359
think the first thing is, just to tell you
flat out, we do not have Census Bureau door-to-door

00:40:46.359 --> 00:40:51.930
survey fidelity on these groups. I wish we
did but we don't.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:58.450
There are by one estimate rendered last year,
some 1,600 of these opposition groups. So

00:40:58.450 --> 00:41:06.420
determining their size and capability and,
importantly, their ideological or political

00:41:06.420 --> 00:41:15.960
leanings is very, very difficult. So all of
these numbers that you hear, we do our best

00:41:15.960 --> 00:41:22.960
from a variety of sources to assess the composition
of these groups and their leanings. But this

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:29.630
is a very, very difficult thing to do.
So there are some portion of our oppositions

00:41:29.630 --> 00:41:37.190
who are regarded as moderate. Moderate these
days is increasingly becoming anyone who's

00:41:37.190 --> 00:41:46.190
not affiliated with ISIL. And so, you know,
we are attempting to engage with them, and

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:51.100
that's the whole point of the train and equip
proposal—project that the Department of

00:41:51.100 --> 00:42:01.729
Defense is gearing up for, is to vet, recruit
and train and equip oppositions in sufficient

00:42:01.729 --> 00:42:07.559
size and capability to actually make a military
difference.

00:42:07.559 --> 00:42:12.239
And so one of our challenges is, again, the
recruiting and vetting part. So we picked

00:42:12.239 --> 00:42:20.859
people that not only are moderate, whatever
that is, but also we have to be sensitive

00:42:20.859 --> 00:42:27.529
to complying with the international rules
of law, which in this environment is a pretty

00:42:27.529 --> 00:42:31.789
tough order.
So this is a very, very complex situation

00:42:31.789 --> 00:42:37.650
in Syria, compounded by the fact that we are
actually not there, which is very different

00:42:37.650 --> 00:42:43.289
than the situation we have in Iraq, where
we are there. And so from an intelligence

00:42:43.289 --> 00:42:48.869
perspective and for our ability to exert leverage
and influence, it's much, much easier in Iraq

00:42:48.869 --> 00:42:57.820
than it is in Syria. That's a long-winded
answer to your question but it's a tough one.

00:42:57.820 --> 00:43:05.120
QUESTION: Ken Roth, from Human Rights Watch.
I'd like to follow up on Iraq. I think at

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:11.089
the policy level there's broad recognition
that the Shia militia and the abuses and the

00:43:11.089 --> 00:43:16.170
sectarian way in which they operated was a
big reason why so many of the Sunni tribes

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:21.250
would join with the U.S. to defeat al-Qaeda
in Iraq, either by neutral or actually joining

00:43:21.250 --> 00:43:26.339
with ISIS because they were so fearful of
the Shia militia. And that was part of why

00:43:26.339 --> 00:43:30.740
Maliki's departure was orchestrated.
Today the Human Rights Watch people on the

00:43:30.740 --> 00:43:35.329
ground still find the Shia militia engaging
in what you might call ethnic cleansing as

00:43:35.329 --> 00:43:42.200
they take various Sunni villages. My question
to you is, what are the prospects of a nonsectarian

00:43:42.200 --> 00:43:47.259
and rights-respecting Iraqi military emerging?
When do you see that happening so that Iraq

00:43:47.259 --> 00:43:50.859
isn't dependent on the Shia militia?
CLAPPER: Well, I think you have to start from

00:43:50.859 --> 00:43:58.769
the premise that Iraq is inherently sectarian.
That's just the nature of the country. The

00:43:58.769 --> 00:44:05.781
country of Iraq is somewhat of an artificial
creation going back to colonial days. And

00:44:05.781 --> 00:44:13.460
so you have the Kurds and then the Sunnis
in the north predominantly and Shias in the

00:44:13.460 --> 00:44:17.140
south.
And you're quite right. The reason that ISIL

00:44:17.140 --> 00:44:23.489
was so successful, yes, it had to do with
their capabilities and prowess on the battlefield,

00:44:23.489 --> 00:44:32.219
but they were dealing with a receptive constituency
in northern Iraq because of the intense hatred

00:44:32.219 --> 00:44:43.920
of the Maliki government, which of course
is imposing the Shia view on them.

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:53.289
And so the challenge for Abadi, who, you know,
does I think recognize what he has to do in

00:44:53.289 --> 00:44:59.289
terms of more inclusiveness of the Sunnis,
because I really don't think there's any way

00:44:59.289 --> 00:45:05.670
of reversing or changing the picture fundamentally
in Iraq unless the Sunnis are included, and

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:10.710
unless that's done in a way that's compelling
and convincing to them, and so far that's

00:45:10.710 --> 00:45:15.609
been a struggle. Abadi has a challenge, the
political challenge of trying to keep his

00:45:15.609 --> 00:45:24.049
Shia base in line with him and supportive
of him. So he has got a very difficult balancing

00:45:24.049 --> 00:45:27.200
act.
Then you have the complication of the Iranians,

00:45:27.200 --> 00:45:34.569
who want a Shia-friendly government in Baghdad
that they feel they can influence because

00:45:34.569 --> 00:45:40.329
it's right next door to them. So he has a
confluence here of a whole bunch of competing

00:45:40.329 --> 00:45:48.530
and conflicting forces that he has to deal
with. And it is a very tough issue. But certainly

00:45:48.530 --> 00:45:56.920
he, I think, is in a better position to do
that than certainly his predecessor.

00:45:56.920 --> 00:46:07.479
QUESTION: Bettye Musham, Duke Islamic Studies
Center founder. Have you had any conversations

00:46:07.479 --> 00:46:13.220
or concerns about the Saudi's funding all
of these madrassas which are promoting the

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:19.140
Wahhabi doctrine of conservatism? And I know
in Turkey there are over 500 of them over

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:27.490
a period of time.
CLAPPER: That is a concern, and I think there

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:33.910
is change afoot in the Mideast. It's not going
to occur overnight, but I think, you know,

00:46:33.910 --> 00:46:43.150
these brutalities, publicized brutalities
by ISIL, beheadings and immolations and the

00:46:43.150 --> 00:46:49.469
like, have really had a galvanizing effect,
even in the Mideast. And so I think it is

00:46:49.469 --> 00:46:58.349
bringing about a change.
One thing we see is the donations to extremist

00:46:58.349 --> 00:47:04.460
groups from many of these countries, donors
in the countries, are declining. Some of that

00:47:04.460 --> 00:47:17.020
is through government activity, government
oversight and with our help. But in the end

00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:23.849
this is about—I hate to use a cliche from
my war, southeast Asia, but it is about the

00:47:23.849 --> 00:47:27.329
hearts and minds here.
And ultimately that is what's going to make

00:47:27.329 --> 00:47:36.609
a difference here, is the narrative or counter
narrative, starting with education. And so

00:47:36.609 --> 00:47:40.390
I think you are quite right to highlight that.
I do think, though, that there is a change

00:47:40.390 --> 00:47:43.999
afoot, but it is not going to happen by close
of business next Friday.

00:47:43.999 --> 00:47:48.220
TOWNSEND: Director Clapper, can you just give
us a sense of scale? You said that the donations

00:47:48.220 --> 00:47:54.559
from the Gulf countries are declining. I'm
not asking for precise numbers off the top

00:47:54.559 --> 00:47:59.009
of your head but give us an order of magnitude.
CLAPPER: I really can't do that because it

00:47:59.009 --> 00:48:04.309
varies from place to place and it varies from
who they are donating to and all this sort

00:48:04.309 --> 00:48:08.569
of thing. I will just make the statement in
general, there has been a big decline. The

00:48:08.569 --> 00:48:17.289
amount of donations that ISIL got was a very,
very—less than 1 percent of their total

00:48:17.289 --> 00:48:28.969
revenue from last year. I can say that.
QUESTION: In recent days the government reauthorized

00:48:28.969 --> 00:48:33.789
the telephone metadata collection program
through June 1st, when there is the sunset

00:48:33.789 --> 00:48:39.089
date obviously of section 215 of the Patriot
Act. What do you want to see happen after

00:48:39.089 --> 00:48:44.099
that?
CLAPPER: Well, what we've agreed to, Attorney

00:48:44.099 --> 00:48:51.880
General Eric Holder and I last September signed
a letter saying that we supported the notion

00:48:51.880 --> 00:49:00.910
of moving the retention of the data to providers,
in a bill that actually came out of the Senate

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:05.109
from Senator Leahy. So we signed up to that.
I think that's the only thing that's realistic

00:49:05.109 --> 00:49:12.170
if we are going to have this at all.
You know, in the end, the Congress giveth

00:49:12.170 --> 00:49:17.950
and the Congress taketh away. So if the Congress
in its wisdom decides that the candle isn't

00:49:17.950 --> 00:49:22.980
worth&nbsp;the flame, the juice isn't worth&nbsp;the
squeeze, whatever metaphor you want to use,

00:49:22.980 --> 00:49:28.549
that's fine. And the intelligence community
will do all we can within the law to do what

00:49:28.549 --> 00:49:33.209
we can to protect the country.
But, I have to say that, you know, every time

00:49:33.209 --> 00:49:41.390
we lose another tool in our toolkit, you know,
it raises the risk. If that tool is taken

00:49:41.390 --> 00:49:49.119
away from us, 215, and some untoward incident
happens which could have been thwarted had

00:49:49.119 --> 00:49:55.589
we had it, I just hope that everyone involved
in that decision assumes responsibility. And

00:49:55.589 --> 00:49:59.509
it not be blamed, if we have another failure,
exclusively on the intelligence community.

00:49:59.509 --> 00:50:04.140
QUESTION: And just to be clear, with the private
providers maintaining that data, do you feel

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:10.789
you've lost an important tool?
CLAPPER: Not necessarily. It will depend,

00:50:10.789 --> 00:50:19.069
though, on, for one, retention period. I think
given the attitude today with providers, they

00:50:19.069 --> 00:50:22.609
will probably do all they can to minimize
the retention period, which of course from

00:50:22.609 --> 00:50:27.471
our standpoint lessens the utility of the
data because you do need some—and we can

00:50:27.471 --> 00:50:35.420
prove this statistically—you do need some
historical data in order to discern a pattern.

00:50:35.420 --> 00:50:45.069
And again, you know, 215 to me is much like
my fire insurance policy. You know, my house

00:50:45.069 --> 00:50:54.430
has never burned down but every year I buy
fire insurance just 

00:50:54.430 --> 00:50:56.780
in case.
QUESTION: Celia Mendoza, Voice of America.

00:50:56.780 --> 00:51:00.890
You mentioned that there is a lot of threats
that we have to be aware of. Venezuela right

00:51:00.890 --> 00:51:06.789
now is in a very dire situation and they have
close relationships with Iran, with Syria,

00:51:06.789 --> 00:51:11.380
as well as with Russia. How do you see the
relationship between the United States and

00:51:11.380 --> 00:51:16.039
the intelligence you are gathering, especially
with all the accusations made by the government

00:51:16.039 --> 00:51:23.950
of Nicolas Maduro against the United States?
CLAPPER: Well, Venezuela is going through

00:51:23.950 --> 00:51:31.369
a difficult time right now, for lots of reasons.
I think economically obviously the price of

00:51:31.369 --> 00:51:40.329
oil has had, because of their disproportionate
dependence on oil revenue, mostly from us,

00:51:40.329 --> 00:51:48.989
that's had an impact on their economy. President
Maduro, maybe not the most astute head of

00:51:48.989 --> 00:51:56.650
state, I'll put it that way, is having trouble
coping with this because of the economic stresses

00:51:56.650 --> 00:52:00.799
it's putting on his country. And of course
what he's most concerned about is people turning

00:52:00.799 --> 00:52:15.859
out in the streets, which would be potentially
a real threat 

00:52:15.859 --> 00:52:21.279
to them.
QUESTION: Sarah Leah Whitson at Human Rights

00:52:21.279 --> 00:52:26.660
Watch. I wanted to ask you some more about
the metanarrative, and in particular the extent

00:52:26.660 --> 00:52:32.160
to which you think the U.S. is really best
placed to take on a metanarrative that will

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:39.619
appeal to people who possibly support Islamist
extremist politics. And also about the metanarrative

00:52:39.619 --> 00:52:45.920
of some of the allies in the anti-ISIS coalition,
like Saudi Arabia, which models a lot of the

00:52:45.920 --> 00:52:50.450
behavior, like beheadings and jailing and
flogging of journalists that ISIS and al-Qaeda

00:52:50.450 --> 00:52:53.109
actually copy.
And I wonder whether there has been any effort

00:52:53.109 --> 00:52:57.799
to influence the metanarrative of the important
partner, Saudi Arabia.

00:52:57.799 --> 00:53:02.849
CLAPPER: Well, of course. We have, and I think
the question raises a good point about the

00:53:02.849 --> 00:53:17.029
extent to which we, the United States, can&nbsp;lead
a narrative, given the general lack of popularity

00:53:17.029 --> 00:53:23.319
of the United States in many of these countries.
I was quite struck with the interview I saw

00:53:23.319 --> 00:53:31.049
yesterday of King Abdullah of Jordan, who
I have great respect for. And I think he said

00:53:31.049 --> 00:53:37.140
some things worth considering, worth thinking
about, which is essentially "this is kind

00:53:37.140 --> 00:53:40.369
of our thing. This is something we have to
do".

00:53:40.369 --> 00:53:47.530
And I don't think he intended it in just a
military context, but in the context of getting

00:53:47.530 --> 00:53:53.630
ahead of or countering the larger narrative
here. And you know, we are doing this in all

00:53:53.630 --> 00:53:59.989
kinds of ways to try to counter the narrative
but I think in the end, you know, our ability

00:53:59.989 --> 00:54:08.499
to do that and to really influence change
in attitudes and change in worldview is only

00:54:08.499 --> 00:54:15.079
going to come about when the people there
themselves do that.

00:54:15.079 --> 00:54:26.779
QUESTION: Paula DiPerna, NTR Foundation. Thank
you very much for your service. And the question

00:54:26.779 --> 00:54:35.459
is, with—beyond the sort of local assets,
people who obviously you have to remunerate,

00:54:35.459 --> 00:54:40.489
can you speak to the degree to which intelligence
gathering has been privatized, outsourced,

00:54:40.489 --> 00:54:44.249
contracted out, both in terms of percentage
and dollar value?

00:54:44.249 --> 00:54:51.430
CLAPPER: Well, actually it's declined. At
least as a formal part of the intelligence

00:54:51.430 --> 00:54:56.921
community, that which we've contracted out
or hired contractors has declined by 40 percent

00:54:56.921 --> 00:55:04.369
since 2007, and in our 2016 submission it
is going to go down more. So we are less and

00:55:04.369 --> 00:55:10.869
less dependent on contractors.
This of course is the classical historical

00:55:10.869 --> 00:55:15.880
cycle that we go through in the intelligence
community, where the pendulum is swinging

00:55:15.880 --> 00:55:23.499
back from the mushrooming that we did right
after 9/11, where the intelligence community,

00:55:23.499 --> 00:55:28.230
which had been shrinking for five years, suddenly
expanded. And the fast way to do that, of

00:55:28.230 --> 00:55:31.609
course, is to hire a lot of contractors, which
we did.

00:55:31.609 --> 00:55:38.990
So now that our funding is going down, and
in the interest of trying to protect our government

00:55:38.990 --> 00:55:43.029
workforce, which I feel very strongly about
protecting these great people, young people

00:55:43.029 --> 00:55:50.640
that we've brought on since 9/11, so we are
peeling off contractors, so it's actually

00:55:50.640 --> 00:56:01.089
declining.
QUESTION: Jeff Laurenti. I wonder, Mr. Director,

00:56:01.089 --> 00:56:07.650
if you could comment on the love triangle
between American, Afghan and Pakistani intelligence

00:56:07.650 --> 00:56:13.699
as the United States is disengaging. How reliable
is the Afghan intelligence network in terms

00:56:13.699 --> 00:56:18.079
of its penetration of the Taliban? Do you
see any signs that the Taliban are beginning

00:56:18.079 --> 00:56:26.190
to feel demoralization? And how genuine is
the ISI in Pakistan's seeming, slow-creep

00:56:26.190 --> 00:56:29.630
embrace of normal relations with the Afghan
government?

00:56:29.630 --> 00:56:39.239
CLAPPER: Well, those are a great bunch of
questions there. I start this from the premise

00:56:39.239 --> 00:56:47.989
that every country, no matter who it is—and
I have dealt with a lot of them from an intelligence

00:56:47.989 --> 00:56:55.260
perspective—they start from the premise
of what is in their national interest. That

00:56:55.260 --> 00:57:02.049
may or may not comport with what we like,
necessarily. And so we have to recognize the

00:57:02.049 --> 00:57:10.200
reality of how things are in the world.
So in the case of the Pakistanis, penultimately

00:57:10.200 --> 00:57:17.540
what motivates their policies, to include
their conduct of intelligence, is what they

00:57:17.540 --> 00:57:24.680
consider their existential threat, which is
India. And so that governs a lot of what they

00:57:24.680 --> 00:57:30.099
do.
Now I will tell you, in line with what I said

00:57:30.099 --> 00:57:38.029
earlier about the public savagery and brutality
of ISIL, the attack on the Pakistani army

00:57:38.029 --> 00:57:44.999
school in Peshawar had a galvanizing impact
on public opinion in Pakistan. And that in

00:57:44.999 --> 00:57:51.259
turn of course influences the government and
influences ISI.

00:57:51.259 --> 00:57:57.739
You know, we had a very low point in our relations
with Pakistan and, you know, as a result of

00:57:57.739 --> 00:58:05.729
the Abbottabad raid in May of 2011, and since
then it has gradually come up. I think the

00:58:05.729 --> 00:58:17.229
Pakistanis realize the impact of when we depart
from Afghanistan and that they need to be

00:58:17.229 --> 00:58:26.219
more active and viable partner, and they are.
There has been a great improvement in their

00:58:26.219 --> 00:58:32.799
approach and their cooperation with us and
their openness. But there are certain things

00:58:32.799 --> 00:58:38.559
that they probably fundamentally are not going
to change, again because of what drives them.

00:58:38.559 --> 00:58:46.019
With respect to Afghanistan, I think the intelligence
services there, particularly the national

00:58:46.019 --> 00:58:53.891
one, is good and getting better. We've spent
a lot of time and effort at enhancing their

00:58:53.891 --> 00:59:00.849
capabilities, but they too are going to behave
in what they believe are their best interests.

00:59:00.849 --> 00:59:10.470
I might say with respect to Afghanistan that
I think I would commend to you history. In

00:59:10.470 --> 00:59:18.709
our last national intelligence estimate we
devoted an annex to the Soviet experience.

00:59:18.709 --> 00:59:26.130
And the lesson learned here is that when they
left in 1989—they continued very robust

00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:30.579
financial support to the Afghan government
to the tune of $3 [billion] to $6 billion

00:59:30.579 --> 00:59:37.219
a year normalized I think in 2011 dollars.
When they left, when that support stopped

00:59:37.219 --> 00:59:43.559
on the 31st of December, 1991, about four
months later the government of Afghanistan

00:59:43.559 --> 00:59:48.569
collapsed.
So the big lesson here is not so much, you

00:59:48.569 --> 00:59:54.430
know, the pace of our drawdown. It's what
happens afterwards in terms of external donors'

00:59:54.430 --> 00:59:58.799
financial support which the Afghan government
is so highly dependent on.

00:59:58.799 --> 01:00:03.240
TOWNSEND: Director Clapper, I think that it
is 2 o'clock and I want to be respectful of

01:00:03.240 --> 01:00:07.681
your time. I want to thank you for being here
with us, and just on a personal note, let

01:00:07.681 --> 01:00:10.920
me thank you for your friendship, your mentorship,
and for your public service.

01:00:10.920 --> 01:00:19.719
CLAPPER: May I say one last thing. It is a
great honor for me to be here in this series

01:00:19.719 --> 01:00:24.539
in light of who it was named after. And the
family is here today, and I just want to say

01:00:24.539 --> 01:00:30.730
they epitomize, and Ken did, the spirit of
so many people who serve in the intelligence

01:00:30.730 --> 01:00:35.160
community today who came to the intelligence
community—came back to the intelligence

01:00:35.160 --> 01:00:40.969
community, as I did, after 9/11.
And so I do want to take note of Ken and his

01:00:40.969 --> 01:00:44.839
tremendous legacy, and it is an honor to be
here in a series named after him. So thanks.

01:00:44.839 --> 01:00:45.219
(APPLAUSE)
END

